  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 requires a fire risk assessment for most non-domestic premises in England and Wales</li><li>The duty sits with the responsible person, who must ensure the assessment is suitable and sufficient</li><li>PAS 79 is a widely used methodology but is not the only valid route and is not law itself</li><li>The assessment must record significant findings and an action plan; that record is what enforcers and insurers want to see</li><li>Software does not make an assessment valid; a competent assessor does</li></ul></div><h2>What the Fire Safety Order requires</h2><p>The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 is the principal fire safety legislation for non-domestic premises in England and Wales. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own equivalent regimes. The Order places a duty on the responsible person, usually the employer, owner or occupier, to carry out and maintain a fire risk assessment and put in place appropriate fire precautions.</p><p>The assessment must identify the fire hazards, the people at risk, and the measures needed to remove or reduce that risk. Where five or more people are employed, or a licence or alteration notice is in force, the significant findings must be recorded. Enforcement sits with the local fire and rescue authority. This is a legal summary, not legal advice; check the current requirements for your premises and nation.</p><h2>PAS 79 and the assessment structure</h2><p>PAS 79 is a publicly available specification setting out a methodology for fire risk assessment. Many competent assessors follow it because it gives a recognised, defensible structure: gather building and occupancy information, identify hazards and people at risk, evaluate existing fire safety measures, and reach a judgement on the remaining risk with an action plan. PAS 79 is not the law and not the only acceptable approach. The Order requires a suitable and sufficient assessment without mandating a method. A structured method stops the assessor skipping a hazard or an occupant group under time pressure.</p><h2>Significant findings and the action plan</h2><p>The real product of a fire risk assessment is the record of significant findings and the action plan that follows. A significant finding is a hazard or shortfall that materially affects fire safety: a wedged fire door, a blocked escape route, missing detection, combustible storage in a stairwell. Each needs recording clearly, with a priority and a responsible owner. On-site capture pays off here: a finding photographed and described at the fire door is unambiguous, while the same finding recalled at the office risks vagueness. Quickler builds the action plan as each significant finding is recorded and presents it as a clear, prioritised list.</p><h2>Why capture on site</h2><ul><li><strong>Defensible record:</strong> findings photographed at the fire door beat findings recalled at the desk</li><li><strong>Structured method:</strong> a PAS 79 shaped prompt means no hazard or occupant group is skipped</li><li><strong>Action plan builds live:</strong> significant findings become a prioritised list as the assessor walks</li><li><strong>Clear output:</strong> the responsible person receives a report they can act on and evidence to insurers or enforcers</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> managers and admins who read reports cost nothing to add. Quickler charges per report with unlimited users</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> software does not make a fire risk assessment suitable and sufficient; a competent assessor working to the Fire Safety Order does. Quickler does not certify anything and is not a replica of a specific insurer's or scheme's form. This is a general summary of the framework and general reporting software, not legal advice; the responsible person and assessor must satisfy themselves the assessment meets current legal requirements. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if a specific format requirement affects your work.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What law requires a fire risk assessment in the UK?</h3><p>In England and Wales, the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 requires a fire risk assessment for most non-domestic premises. The duty sits with the responsible person, usually the employer, owner or occupier, who must ensure the assessment is suitable and sufficient and act on its findings. Scotland and Northern Ireland have their own equivalent regimes.</p><h3>What is PAS 79?</h3><p>PAS 79 is a publicly available specification setting out a widely used methodology for fire risk assessment. It gives a recognised, defensible structure covering building information, hazards, people at risk, existing measures and an action plan. It is not the law and not the only acceptable approach; the Fire Safety Order requires a suitable and sufficient assessment without mandating one method.</p><h3>Does a fire risk assessment have to be written down?</h3><p>The significant findings must be recorded where five or more people are employed, or where a licence or alteration notice applies. In practice most premises benefit from a written record of significant findings and an action plan, because that is what enforcing authorities and insurers ask to see. This is a general summary, not legal advice for your specific premises.</p><h3>Can I use Quickler for fire risk assessments?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler delivers the assessment as a WhatsApp conversation, and its workflow can be built to follow a PAS 79 shaped structure. The assessor answers each question with text, voice note or photo, and the report generates as a clean PDF and web record with a prioritised action plan. There is no app to install.</p><h3>Does Quickler make my fire risk assessment legally valid?</h3><p>No. A competent assessor working to the Fire Safety Order makes the assessment suitable and sufficient. Quickler produces the report and the action plan; it does not certify the assessment or replace professional judgement, and it is general reporting software, not legal advice.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/insurance-survey-software-uk.html\">Insurance survey software (overview)</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/property-risk-assessment-insurance-uk.html\">Property risk assessment for insurers</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/insurance-risk-survey-report-uk.html\">Commercial insurance risk survey reports</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/loss-adjuster-site-report-app-uk.html\">Loss adjuster site report app</a></li></ul>",
